== Characteristic classes <sec:characteristic-classes>
#idx("characteristic", "classes", target: [@sec:characteristic-classes])#idx(
  "class",
  "characteristic",
  target: [@sec:characteristic-classes],
)

In this section we introduce cohomology classes associated to vector bundles.
Real and complex representations of a group $G$ give rise to vector bundles over
$B G$ in a natural way, and so we obtain cohomology classes associated to
representations.

We start by describing the cohomology of
$B GL_n (CC) = B U(n) = G^n (CC^infinity)$. If $n = 1$ we have $U(1) = S^1$ and
$B U(1) = B S^1 = CC P^infinity$. It is not hard to calculate that
$H^* (CC P^infinity; ZZ)$ is a polynomial ring $ZZ[x]$ on a generator $x$ in
degree two. A choice of sign for $x$ corresponds to a choice of orientation for
the sphere $S^2 = CC P^1 subset.eq CC P^infinity$. We choose the sign to
correspond to the natural orientation coming from the complex structure.

For a general $n$, we have an inclusion $(S^1)^n subset.eq U(n)$ as the diagonal
matrices, and hence a map $phi.alt : (CC P^infinity)^n -> B U(n)$. This induces
a map on cohomology rings
$ phi.alt^* : H^* (B U(n); ZZ) -> H^* ((CC P^infinity)^n; ZZ). $
The right hand side is a polynomial ring in generators $x_1, dots, x_n$ of
degree two. Since $(S^1)^n$ is contained in the group of monomial matrices in
$U(n)$, the image of $phi.alt^*$ lies in the invariants of the symmetric group
$Sigma_n$ permuting #source(61)the $n$ copies of $CC P^infinity$, namely in the
subring $ZZ[sigma_1, dots, sigma_n] subset.eq ZZ[x_1, dots, x_n]$ generated by
the elementary symmetric functions $sigma_i (x_1, dots, x_n)$ of degree $2i$.

It turns out that $phi.alt^*$ is injective, and its image exactly equals
$ZZ[sigma_1, dots, sigma_n]$. For a proof, see Husemoller [@bib:Husemoller1975],
Section~18.3 or Milnor and Stasheff [@bib:Milnor1974], Section~14.3. We define
$c_i in H^(2i) (B U(n); ZZ)$ to be the preimage of the element
$sigma_i in H^(2i) ((CC P^infinity)^n; ZZ)$ under $phi.alt^*$, so that
$ H^* (B U(n); ZZ) = ZZ[c_1, dots, c_n]. $
#idx("cohomology", "of $B U(n)$")Now if $xi$ is a complex vector bundle over a
paracompact base space $B$, with classifying map
$f : B -> G^n (CC^infinity) = B U(n)$ (see Theorem~@th:complex-vector-bundles),
we define the *Chern classes*#idx(
  "Chern",
  "class",
)#idx("class", "Chern") of $xi$ to be
$ c_i (xi) = f^* (c_i) in H^(2i) (B; ZZ) $
for $1 lt.eq i lt.eq n$, and $c_i (xi) = 0$ if $i > n$. The *total Chern
class*#idx("total", "Chern class") of $xi$ is defined to be the inhomogeneous
element
$ c(xi) = 1 + c_1 (xi) + dots.c + c_n (xi) in H^* (B; ZZ). $

#proposition[
  Chern classes of complex vector bundles over paracompact base spaces are
  characterised by the following properties:
  + (Naturality) If $rho : B' -> B$ is a map of base spaces and $xi$ is a vector
    bundle over $B$, then
    $c_i (rho^* (xi)) = rho^* (c_i (xi)) in H^(2i) (B'; ZZ)$.
  + $c(xi_1 plus.o xi_2) = c(xi_1).c(xi_2)$.
  + If $gamma$ is the canonical bundle over $G^1 (CC^infinity) = CC P^infinity$
    then $c_1 (gamma)$ is the canonical generator (called $x$ above) for
    $H^2 (CC P^infinity; ZZ)$.
] <prop:chern-classes>

#proof[
  Properties (i) and (iii) are clear from the definition. Property (ii) follows
  from the observation that $1 + c_1 + dots.c + c_n in H^* (B U(n); ZZ)$ has
  image $product_(i=1)^n (1 + x_i)$ in $H^* ((CC P^infinity)^n; ZZ)$ and this
  expression is clearly multiplicative over sums of bundles.

  To show that these properties characterise the Chern classes, we argue as
  follows. If $c'_i$ are classes satisfying (i), (ii) and (iii), then by
  property (iii) $c'_i$ agrees with $c_i$ for the canonical bundle on
  $CC P^infinity$. Hence by properties (i) and (ii), $c'_i$ agrees with $c_i$
  for the sum of the canonical bundles on $(CC P^infinity)^n$. Applying property
  (i) to the map $phi.alt : (CC P^infinity)^n -> B U(n)$, we see that $c'_i$
  agrees with $c_i$ for the canonical bundle on $B U(n)$. Now by applying
  property (i) to the classifying map, it follows that $c'_i$ agrees with $c_i$
  for an arbitrary bundle over a paracompact base space.
]

Now suppose $G$ is a topological group having the homotopy type of a CW-complex
(for example a discrete group), and suppose $M$ is a finite dimensional complex
representation of $G$. Then $E G times_G M$ is a complex vector bundle over
$B G$, and we may define Chern classes $c_i (M) in H^(2i) (G, ZZ)$ to be the
Chern classes of this vector bundle. The representation $M$ is given by a group
homomorphism $rho : G -> U(n)$, and hence gives rise to a map
$B rho : B G -> B U(n)$. The canonical bundle over $B U(n)$ may be thought of as
#source(62)being obtained by the above construction from the canonical $n$
dimensional representation of $U(n)$, and so by naturality we have
$ c_i (M) = (B rho)^* (c_i) in H^(2i) (G, ZZ). $

The corresponding theory for real vector bundles and real representations goes
as follows. We have $B O(1) = B(ZZ slash 2) = RR P^infinity$, and
$H^* (RR P^infinity; ZZ slash 2)$ is a polynomial ring $ZZ slash 2[x]$ on a
generator $x$ in degree one. We have an inclusion
$(ZZ slash 2)^n = O(1)^n subset.eq O(n)$ as the diagonal matrices, and hence a
map $phi.alt : (RR P^infinity)^n -> B O(n)$. The induced map on cohomology
$
  phi.alt^* : H^* (B O(n); ZZ slash 2) -> H^* ((RR P^infinity)^n; ZZ slash 2)
  = ZZ slash 2[x_1, dots, x_n]
$
is injective, and again the image is the subring
$ ZZ slash 2[sigma_1, dots, sigma_n] subset.eq ZZ slash 2[x_1, dots, x_n] $
generated by the elementary polynomials $sigma_i (x_1, dots, x_n)$ of degree
$i$. See for example Husemoller [@bib:Husemoller1975], Section~18.5 or Milnor
and Stasheff [@bib:Milnor1974], Section~7. We define
$w_i in H^i (B O(n); ZZ slash 2)$ to be the preimage of
$sigma_i in H^i ((RR P^infinity)^n; ZZ slash 2)$ under $phi.alt^*$, so that
$ H^* (B O(n); ZZ slash 2) = ZZ slash 2[w_1, dots, w_n]. $
#idx("cohomology", "of $B O(n)$")Now if $xi$ is a real vector bundle over a
paracompact base space $B$, with classifying map
$f : B -> G^n (RR^infinity) = B O(n)$ (see Theorem~@th:real-vector-bundles), we
define the *Stiefel–Whitney classes*#idx("class", "Stiefel–Whitney")#idx(
  "Stiefel",
  "–Whitney class",
) of $xi$ to be
$ w_i (xi) = f^* (w_i) in H^i (B; ZZ slash 2) $
for $1 lt.eq i lt.eq n$, and $w_i (xi) = 0$ if $i > n$. The *total
Stiefel–Whitney class*#idx("total", "Stiefel–Whitney class") of $xi$ is defined
to be
$ w(xi) = 1 + w_1 (xi) + dots.c + w_n (xi) in H^* (B; ZZ slash 2). $

#proposition[
  The Stiefel–Whitney classes of real vector bundles over paracompact base
  spaces are characterised by the following properties:
  + (Naturality) If $rho : B' -> B$ is a map of base spaces and $xi$ is a vector
    bundle over $B$, then
    $w_i (rho^* (xi)) = rho^* (w_i (xi)) in H^i (B'; ZZ slash 2)$.
  + $w(xi_1 plus.o xi_2) = w(xi_1).w(xi_2)$.
  + If $gamma$ is the canonical bundle over $G^1 (RR^infinity) = RR P^infinity$
    then $w_1 (gamma)$ is the canonical generator (called $x$ above) for
    $H^1 (RR P^infinity; ZZ slash 2)$.
] <prop:stiefel-whitney-classes>

#proof[
  The proof exactly parallels the proof in the complex case.
]

If $G$ is a topological group having the homotopy type of a CW-complex, and $M$
is a finite dimensional real representation of $G$, then $E G times_G M$ is a
real vector bundle over $B G$, and we define Stiefel–Whitney classes
$w_i (M) in H^i (G, ZZ slash 2)$ to be the Stiefel–Whitney classes of this
vector bundle. If the representation $M$ is given by a group homomorphism
$rho : G -> O(n)$, then just as before we have
$ w_i (M) = (B rho)^* (w_i) in H^i (G, ZZ slash 2). $
